<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parliament of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century–<a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Scottish Parliament (pre-1707), Estates of Scotland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was the national legislature of the Kingdom of Scotland from the thirteenth century until 1707. It met as a single chamber of three estates, clergy, nobility and burgh commissioners, much of whose business was prepared by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-of-the-articles">Lords of the Articles</a>. It grew more assertive after 1689 and in 1707 ratified the Treaty of Union, which ended it; a new Scottish Parliament was opened in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland.jpg" alt="Parliament of Scotland" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Parliament grew out of the king's council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and earls. It is perhaps first identifiable in 1235, under Alexander II, when a colloquium already had a political and judicial role. Burgh representatives are first mentioned taking part in decisions in 1296, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> began calling burgh commissioners regularly. Its consent was needed for taxation, and it had a strong influence over justice, foreign policy and war. Before about 1500 General Councils, and afterwards Conventions of Estates, could also handle taxation and legislation, but lacked the full authority of a parliament. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> it met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> and many other towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the death of Alexander III the Parliament gave legitimacy to the Guardians who ran the kingdom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">John Balliol</a> used it to resist the claims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>. Under David II it repeatedly stopped the king from accepting an English succession to the throne, and it arguably reached the height of its power over the Crown after James I returned from English captivity in 1424. It was called less often by the early 16th century and survived in part because of the minorities and regencies that followed 1513.</p><p>In the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a> took power and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> from kirk and Parliament, and after the Anglo-Scots War of 1649-1651 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was joined to the English Protectorate. An independent Parliament was restored in 1661 and annulled the legislation passed since 1633; when it refused to pass the measures of King James, who succeeded in 1685, he suspended it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> it removed the clergy in 1689 and abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-of-the-articles">Lords of the Articles</a> in 1690, and from then until 1707 political parties and alliances formed within it. Older historians called it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> stamp for royal decisions; modern research has found that it played an active role in Scottish affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Lord Chancellor presided, and an act of 1428 creating a common speaker came to nothing. Members met in one chamber, after 1639 in Parliament House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, arranged in a square facing the throne; as the highest court in the kingdom it had an inner bar before the throne for the accused. Some historians identify a fourth estate of shire commissioners and a fifth of officers of state, a view that remains disputed. The term of a Parliament began and ended with the Riding of Parliament, a procession along the Royal Mile from Holyroodhouse, held for the last time on 6 May 1703.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parliament of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Parliament of Scotland</b>, a single chamber of the estates of clergy, nobility and burgh commissioners, developed from the thirteenth century; much of its business was prepared by a committee, the Lords of the Articles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
